\title{Bisimulation versus Isomorphism as Semantic Resolution:\\ Multiplicity, Sharing, and the Naming Budget}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{3 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We study the exact resolving power of modal observation on a class of transition systems that is automatically image-finite and converse well-founded: \emph{descending tag-indexed frames}, whose worlds are the natural numbers and in which a world $m$ may step, at any tag $i$, only to worlds $n < m$.

We prove a Hennessy--Milner theorem: bisimilarity and modal equivalence coincide, with no finiteness hypothesis beyond the descending condition. Consequently an interpretation of pointed models is invariant under all modal observations if and only if it is invariant under bisimulation, so every observationally invariant quantity factors through the modal theory and through nothing finer. We exhibit the full observational hierarchy
\[
\DepthInv_0 \subsetneq \DepthInv_1 \subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq \ModalInv = \BisimInv \subsetneq \IsoInv,
\]
with explicit separating observations: the height formulas $\Box^{k+1}\bot$ below, and the out-degree above.

Our principal negative result refutes the natural conjecture that the top gap is characterized by multiplicity-sensitive observations. The \emph{shared diamond} $5\to3,4$; $3\to1$; $4\to1$ and its unravelling $5\to3,4$; $3\to1$; $4\to2$ are bisimilar, have equal out-degrees at every related pair and every tag, and are nevertheless non-isomorphic. The correct statement is a two-step ladder whose second gap is measured by \emph{sharing} rather than multiplicity.

We then identify what does close the gap and quantify its cost: nominal valuations collapse modal equivalence to equality of worlds already in the atomic fragment; binary naming does the same for all worlds below $2^k$ using $k$ atoms; and a pigeonhole argument shows no valuation with $k$ atoms can atomically separate $2^k+1$ worlds, so the threshold is exactly $\lceil\log_2 N\rceil$. We determine where the depth ladder stops on a fixed truncation --- on worlds of height at most $k$, depth-$k$ agreement implies full modal equivalence, sharply --- and derive a proof-theoretic corollary: the truncated G\"odel--L\"ob-style validity theories of these frames, with their entire depth-restricted reflection spectra, are bisimulation invariants, so no deductive strength distinguishes a shared diamond from its unravelling.
\end{abstract}

